why 2 1/2 DOUBLING Alto (F♯–E) and tenor (D–E) meet on E by step from opposite sides.
why 4 1/2 SEVENTH The tenor's F rises to G while the bass takes E, the note F would fall to. The resolution moves to the bass, which steps F–E.
why 4 5/8 CROSSING The tune passes down C–B–A while the alto holds C above it for an eighth: a tiny crossing inside one beat.
why 4 3/4 OVERLAP The alto drops a fifth to F, below where the tenor just was, while the tenor falls to C. They move down together, so the order stays clear.
why 6 0 CROSSING After the fermata the bass starts the new phrase high on C, above the tenor's A. It steps down C–B–A, and the voices are back in order by the next beat.
why 10 1/4 DOUBLING A passing chord over the bass's held D; its doubling follows the moving upper voices.
why 10 1/4 SIX_FOUR Over the bass's held D the upper voices form a passing sound on the way to the G major chord at the fermata, the way a cadential ⁶₄ leads to its V.
why 13 1/2 CADENCE The tune is in the Phrygian mode and ends on E, so Bach ends on the E major chord, which in A minor is V. A Phrygian ending, common in chorales.
